     <article class="story single" data-beat="positive">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">POSITIVE</span> · The Better India · 2026-10-21 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Almora Mechanic's Son Develops eVTOL Prototype After 13 Years of Self-Taught Innovation</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Ravi Tamta</span> <span>#Flying Car</span> <span>#eVTOL</span> <span>#Almora</span></div>
       <p>Ravi Tamta, from Kaflikhan village near Jageswar Dham in Almora, Uttarakhand, has dedicated 13 years to developing HAPIDA SKYNeX, an electric flying vehicle prototype capable of vertical take-off and landing (eVTOL). His innovation was driven by a practical problem: the hours-long journeys required to reach hospitals via winding mountain roads in his region.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-21/almora-mechanics-son-develops-evtol-prototype-after-13-years.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Despite lacking an engineering degree, Tamta pursued his vision by learning from the internet, supported by his parents, and through extensive experimentation. Under his venture, Hapida Sky Private Limited, the HAPIDA SKYNeX successfully completed its maiden test flight in August. The prototype took off from an army helipad in Almora and remained airborne for nearly a minute, marking a significant milestone in its development.</p><p>Utilizing a modified and scaled-up multirotor drone technology combined with an electric propulsion system, the HAPIDA SKYNeX is designed to carry a person. This multirotor arrangement, which incorporates multiple electric motors and propellers, generates the necessary lift for vertical take-off, sustained hovering, and controlled movement through the air. The single-seater prototype features a lightweight frame, a cockpit, and a transparent canopy, and its electric system ensures no direct exhaust emissions during operation.</p><p>Tamta's primary goal is to enhance air travel accessibility in hilly regions, particularly for medical emergencies and areas difficult to reach by road. He envisions that a journey of approximately 40 kilometres, which typically takes 90 minutes by mountain road, could potentially be completed in 10 to 15 minutes by air. While the prototype is still in its early stages and requires further testing for reliable flight control, battery performance, structural stability, and safety protocols, its initial flight represents a forward step for an idea rooted in solving everyday challenges in the mountains.</p><p>Why it matters: This innovation offers a potential solution to improve accessibility and emergency response in remote mountainous terrain.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Comprehensive and well-structured report on Ravi Tamta's eVTOL development.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://thebetterindia.com/innovation/ravi-tamta-electric-tesla-flying-vehicle-almora-uttrakhand-indian-innovation-12695251" rel="noopener">The Better India</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
