     <article class="story single" data-beat="sports">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SPORTS</span> · NDTV Sports · 2026-10-21 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>India's Reputation Alone Insufficient for New Zealand Tour, Says Ravi Shastri</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#India</span> <span>#New Zealand</span> <span>#Cricket</span> <span>#Ravi Shastri</span></div>
       <p>Former head coach Ravi Shastri issued a blunt reminder to the Indian cricket team on Wednesday, stating that reputation alone would not suffice against New Zealand on their home turf. Ahead of the opening T20I on Thursday, Shastri urged the visitors to absorb and then exert pressure on the hosts. The five-match T20I series is scheduled to conclude on November 1.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-21/indias-reputation-alone-insufficient-for-new-zealand-tour-sa.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The tour itinerary also includes a five-match ODI series, which will serve as preparation for the 2027 Men's ODI World Cup. Following this, India will play a two-match Test series in Wellington and Christchurch from November 19 to December 1. Additionally, an India A squad is touring New Zealand for three red-ball matches, commencing on October 24 in Lincoln.</p><p>Shastri, in a statement issued by Sony Sports Network, emphasized New Zealand's home advantage: &quot;New Zealand will come hard at India. They know their conditions, they do their homework and they make you earn every win. India have the firepower, but reputation won't win them this series.&quot; He added, &quot;They have to get out there, absorb the pressure and put it straight back on New Zealand. That is what I'll be watching for from the first T20I on October 22.&quot;</p><p>Former India Test captain Ajinkya Rahane also looked ahead to the Test leg, highlighting that victories in both matches would be crucial for India's aspirations to reach the 2027 World Test Championship (WTC) final, slated for The Oval from June 9-13. Rahane welcomed the return of veteran pacer Bhuvneshwar Kumar, noting his experience would be valuable in the T20Is. He advised the team to focus on each match and adhere to their plans, acknowledging the different challenge New Zealand conditions pose to Indian batters.</p><p>Off-spin great Ravichandran Ashwin described a tour of New Zealand as one of the most demanding assignments in world cricket, stressing a team's need for adaptation. He pointed out the distinct challenges each format presents, from reading T20I conditions to showing patience in Test cricket. Ashwin expressed anticipation for the series, confident in India's quality across formats.</p><p>For the broadcast, Ashwin will provide commentary for the T20Is in English, Hindi, and Tamil. Rahane will join the Hindi line-up for all three formats, while Shastri will be part of the English line-up for the white-ball matches. Former New Zealand players such as Stephen Fleming (T20Is only), Tim Southee, Simon Doull, Shane Bond, and Ian Smith will also contribute their expertise on home conditions throughout the multi-format series.</p><p>What to watch: India's ability to absorb and return pressure in the opening T20I against New Zealand.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Thoroughly covers the source facts including dates, broadcast roles, and quotes.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
