     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · BBC Science · 2026-10-21 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Proposed £3.6bn Tunnel Project Aims to Eliminate Sewage Spills into Windermere</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>United Utilities and environmental campaigners have unveiled a £3.6bn plan to stop sewage discharges into Windermere, England’s largest lake. The proposal, titled &quot;Only Rainwater into Windermere,&quot; involves constructing a tunnel up to 12 miles long to divert sewage away from the lake, which has suffered from seasonal algal blooming caused by nutrient-rich effluent.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-21/proposed-36bn-tunnel-project-aims-to-eliminate-sewage-spills.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The government must now decide whether to approve the long-term project. While United Utilities CEO Louise Beardmore describes the plan as a necessary century-long solution, activist Matt Staniek emphasized that the proposal is currently only a plan and not yet definitive action to stop pollution.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Accurate summary of the proposal, though it omits the specific details about the two potential discharge locations (River Leven or Irish Sea).</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cr5yn4pky527o?at_medium=RSS&amp;at_campaign=rss" rel="noopener">BBC Science</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
